# Shell scripts in this repo are written on Windows and executed on Linux Pis.
#
# git config core.autocrlf is true on the Windows workstation, so without this
# rule every checkout hands the .sh files CRLF endings. bash on the Pi then
# reads the trailing carriage return as part of the command and the script dies
# on its first line:
#
#     /tmp/harden-pi.sh: line 45: $'\r': command not found
#     : invalid option namene 46: set: pipefail
#
# That is exactly as confusing as it looks, and it has already cost a debugging
# session mid-deploy. Force LF for shell scripts regardless of platform.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Deliberately NOT normalising *.py here. Python is indifferent to line endings,
# and rewriting the deployed scripts would change their SHA-256 — which is what
# the integrity checker compares against its pristine baseline on each machine.
# Changing every seeder's hash to tidy up whitespace is not a trade worth making
# while those machines are in production.